Third Session.
Five Policy Questions.

Question: When emperors and kings make plans for the empire and for ten thousand generations, they must establish laws of governance, so that those laws may bestow benefits without end. As for the flourishing kings of the Three Dynasties, the reason their descendants over several hundred years alike achieved order and tranquility was that their laws were preserved; examining the historical records, one can verify this. In later ages, among rulers who exerted themselves in seeking good governance, none surpassed Emperor Xuan of the Han (Han Xuandi 漢宣帝) and Emperor Shenzong of the Song (Song Shenzong 宋神宗). Yet in the former case, the laws continued those of the Qin and the governance mixed in the way of the hegemon, which those who prize the highest antiquity disdain as base and refuse to abide in. Nevertheless, when his ministers set things forth clause by clause and the ruler guarded them as family laws, how did he so readily equal the virtue of the Yin and Zhou? When the Song reached the Yuanfeng 元豐 era (1078–1085), its laws had deteriorated and weakness grew ever greater. What Shenzong and his ministers cited as their models were Yao 堯 and Shun 舜, and the Offices of Zhou (Zhouguan 周官). The New Policies they instituted were not entirely wrong; why then did they brew disaster by the Shaosheng 紹聖 era (1094–1098)? Could it be that it depended on the men and not on the administrative measures? Why did their successes and failures follow such different tracks? Our Grand Progenitor, Emperor Gao (Taizu Gaohuangdi 太祖高皇帝), founded the dynastic enterprise and handed down the succession, far surpassing the hundred kings; his regular institutions were clear and complete, shining brilliantly throughout the realm, …
